Article 22-10 (Tax Credit for Production Costs of Video Content) (1) "National prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the provisions, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 25-6 (1) of the Act means a person who is a video producer defined in subparagraph 14 of Article 2 of the Copyright Act, who meets the requirements prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance (hereinafter referred to as "video content producer"). <Amended on Feb. 29, 2024>
  (2)"Video content prescribed by Presidential Decree" n the provisions, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 25-6 of the Act means anything falling under any of the following subparagraphs (hereafter in this Article referred to as"video content"): <Amended on Feb. 11, 2020; Feb. 28, 2023>
  1. A broadcast program falling under any of the following items, among those defined in subparagraph 17 of Article 2 of the Broadcasting Act:
    (a) Broadcast programs on entertainment under Article 50 (2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Broadcasting Act;
    (b) Documentaries among broadcast programs on culture under Article 50 (2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Broadcasting Act;
    (c) Animation programs broadcast on television by a broadcasting business entity defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Broadcasting Act, among animation programs defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of Act on the Promotion of Animation Industry;
  2. A movie defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Promotion of the Motion Pictures and Video Products Act, which has been continuously screened in a movie theater for a certain period of time or more as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance (hereafter in this Article referred to as"movie");
  3. A video product defined in subparagraph 12 of Article 2 of the Promotion of the Motion Pictures and Video Products Act, which has received a rating under any of the following items and has been made available for viewing through an online video service pursuant to subparagraph 12-2 of Article 2 of the Telecommunications Business Act:
    (a) A rating by the Korea Media Rating Board under Article 50 of the Promotion of the Motion Pictures and Video Products Act;
    (b) A rating by an independent rating business entity under Article 50-2 of the Promotion of the Motion Pictures and Video Products Act.
  (3)"Cost prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the provisions, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 25-6 (1) of the Act means the cost expenses prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, including personnel expenses for the persons participating in the production of video content (hereafter in this Article referred to as"production cost of video content"): Provided, the following expenses shall be excluded herefrom: <Amended on Feb. 15, 2022; Feb. 28, 2023>
  1. Amounts received from the State, local governments, public institutions under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions, and local public enterprises under the Local Public Enterprises Act and used for production cost of video content;
  2. Expenses prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, such as advertising cost or public relations expenses;
  3. Production cost of existing video content for which a tax credit has been already granted if another video content has been produced by utilizing the video content for which a tax credit has been already granted under Article 25-6 of the Act;
  (4) "Video content that satisfies the requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 25-6 (1) 2 (b) of the Act means video content that satisfies all of the following requirements. <Newly Inserted on Feb. 29, 2024>
  1. The proportion of expenses incurred within the Republic of Korea is at least 80 out of 100 of the expenses for filming production prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance;
  2. At least three of the following requirements shall be satisfied:
    (a) The ratio of labor costs paid to Koreans among the labor costs paid to writers and major staff prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance shall be not less than 80/100;
    (b) The ratio of the performance fees paid to Koreans out of the performance fees prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance shall be not less than 80/100;
    (c) The ratio of expenses incurred in Korea among the expenses incurred in post-production as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance shall be at least 80/100;
    (d) The number of rights held by a video content producer shall be at least three among the reproduction rights, public performance rights, broadcasting rights, transmission rights, distribution rights, and derivative works under the Copyright Act. In such cases, specific criteria for determining possession of rights shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
  (5) Where any video content referred to in paragraph (2) 1 or 3 broadcast in a series or made available for viewing through an online video service during multiple taxable years, a tax credit may be applied to the production cost calculated as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance. <Amended on Feb. 28, 2023>
  (6) A person who intends to be granted a tax credit pursuant to Article 25-6 (1) of the Act shall submit an application for tax credit, a statement of tax deductible amount, and other necessary documents in the forms prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, to the head of the competent tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment, along with the return of tax base for the taxable year classified as follows: <Amended on Feb. 28, 2023; Feb. 29, 2024>
  1. In cases of a video content referred to in paragraph (2) 1 or 3: The taxable year in it is first broadcast or made available for viewing through an online video service: Provided, That in cases of paragraph (5), it means either of the following taxable years:
    (a) Each taxable year in which it is broadcast or made available for viewing through an online video service;
    (b) The taxable year in which the last episode of such video content is broadcast or made available for viewing through an online video service;
  2. In cases of a movie: the taxable year in which the movie began to be shown in a movie theater.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Feb. 7, 2017]
  [Moved from Article 22-6 <Feb. 12, 2019>]

Article 22-11 (Tax Credit for Investment of Domestic Corporation in Specialized Cultural Industry Company) (1) "SMEs or middle-standing enterprises prescribed by the Presidential Decree" in the provisions, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 25-7 (1) of the Act refers to SMEs or middle-standing enterprises that have invested in a company specialized in cultural industry under the Framework Act on the Promotion of Cultural Industries (hereafter referred to as "specialized cultural industry company" in this Article): Provided, That this shall exclude SMEs or middle-standing enterprises that are entrusted by a specialized cultural industry company pursuant to Article 51 (1) of the Framework Act on the Promotion of Cultural Industries to produce video content and thus are subject to Article 25-6 (1) of the Act.
  (2) "Video content prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the provisions, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 25-7 (1) of the Act means the video content referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 22-10 (2).
  (3) "Expenses prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 25-7 (1) 2 of the Act means expenses for producing video content under Article 22-10 (3).
  (4) The amount of investment under Article 25-7 (1) 1 of the Act and the manufacturing costs under subparagraph 2 of the same paragraph shall be calculated based on the end date of the business year in which corporate tax is deducted pursuant to the aforesaid paragraph.
  (5) A person who wishes to be granted a tax credit under Article 25-7 (1) of the Act shall file an application for tax credits in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, with the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment, when he/she files a tax return for the relevant business year.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Feb. 29, 2024]

Article 23 (Tax Credits for Employment-Creating Investment) (1)"Investment prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the main clause of Article 26 (1) of the Act, with the exception of its subparagraphs, means an investment made by a national who engages in any business activities other than the consumer service businesses specified in Article 29 (3), to acquire a new facility that is one of the business assets prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance (hereafter in this Article, referred to as"business asset") and to use it for the relevant business. <Amended on Feb. 7, 2017>
  (2) The amount invested under Article 26 (1) of the Act shall be the amount of subparagraph 1 minus the amount of subparagraph 2: <Amended on Feb. 2, 2012>
  1. The greater of the amount calculated by multiplying the total amount invested, by the progress rate of work referred to in Article 69 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act, and the amount actually spent by the relevant taxable year;
  2. The aggregate of the following amounts:
    (a) The amount invested, subject to Article 26 (1) 1 of the Act before the relevant taxable year;
    (b) The amount calculated by applying mutatis mutandis subparagraph 1 to the portion invested before the relevant taxable year, except the amount of item (a).
  (3) Deleted. <Feb. 17, 2021>
  (4)"Service businesses prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the main clause of Article 26 (1) 2 of the Act, with the exception of its items, means any business (hereafter in this Article, referred to as"service businesses"), except the following: <Newly Inserted on Feb. 3, 2015; May 10, 2016; Feb. 7, 2017>
  1. Agriculture, forestry, and fisheries;
  2. Mining;
  3. Manufacturing;
  4. Electricity, gas, steam, and water supply;
  5. Construction;
  6. Consumer service businesses provided for in Article 29 (3);
  (5)"Schools prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 26 (1) 2 (a) of the Act means the following schools (hereinafter referred to as"high schools, etc. aligned to industry demand"): <Amended on Feb. 2, 2012; Feb. 21, 2014; Feb. 3, 2015>
  1. High schools aligned to industry demand under Article 90 (1) 10 of the Enforcement Decree of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act;
  2. Specialized high schools under Article 91 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act;
  3. Various kinds of schools defined in subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act (limited to schools that provide curricula for vocational education, among alternative schools defined in Article 60-3 of that Act, and schools that provide curricula for vocational education under entrustment to students enrolled in general high schools defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 76-2 of the Enforcement Decree of that Act).
  (6) For the purposes of Article 26 (1) 2 of the Act, where a national engaging in at least two different businesses, acquires a business asset that may be used for both a service business and any other business, the business asset shall be deemed a business asset for the business for which the asset is mainly used. <Newly Inserted on Feb. 3, 2015; Feb. 7, 2017>
  (7) The number of graduates from high schools, etc. aligned to industry demand mentioned in Article 26 (1) 2 (a) of the Act shall be determined with the number of full-time employees who have signed an employment contract before the lapse of two years since they graduated from high schools, etc. aligned to industry demand (the maximum number of such workers shall be calculated by subtracting the number of full-time employees in the immediately preceding taxable year from that number of full-time employees in the relevant taxable year). <Amended on Feb. 2, 2012; Feb. 3, 2015>
  (8) The number of youth employees, employees with disabilities, and employees aged at least 60, as referred to in Article 26 (1) 2 (b) of the Act shall be calculated as follows: <Amended on Feb. 21, 2014; Feb. 3, 2015>
  1. Number of youth employees: The number of full-time employees (the maximum number of such employees shall be calculated by subtracting the number of full-time employees in the immediately preceding taxable year and the number of graduates from high schools, etc. aligned to industry demand under paragraph (7), from the number of full-time employees in the relevant taxable period) between the ages of 15 and 29 on the date an employment contract is entered into: Provided, That, if a person has performed the military service specified in any item of Article 27 (1) 1, and his age calculated by subtracting the period of such military service (which shall not exceed six years) from his age as on the date an employment contract is entered into, does not exceed 29 years, the person shall be deemed a youth;
  2. Number of employees with disabilities: The aggregate of the number of full-time employees with disabilities subject to the Act on Welfare of Persons with Disabilities, and the number of full-time employees who are wounded persons under the Act on the Honorable Treatment of and Support for Persons of Distinguished Service to the State (the maximum number of such employees shall be calculated by subtracting the number of full-time employees in the immediately preceding taxable year, the number of graduates from high schools, etc. aligned to industry demand under paragraph (7), and the number of youth employees referred to in subparagraph 1, from the number of full-time employees in the relevant taxable period);
  3. Number of employees aged at least 60: The number of full-time employees who are aged at least 60 on the date an employment contract is entered into (the maximum number of such employees shall be calculated by subtracting the number of full-time employees in the immediately preceding taxable year, the number of graduates from high schools, etc. aligned to industry demand under paragraph (7), the number of youth employees referred to in subparagraph 1, and the number of employees with disabilities referred to in subparagraph 2, from the number of full-time employees in the relevant taxable period).
  (9) The amount of income tax or corporate tax payable under Article 26 (6) of the Act shall be calculated as follows (the amounts of subparagraphs 1 and 2 (referring to the aggregate of items (a) and (b)) shall not exceed the aggregate of tax credits granted under Articles 26 (1) 2 and 144 (3) of the Act within the immediately preceding two taxable years to the taxable year in which the number of full-time employees decreased); and such amount shall be paid as income tax or corporate tax, at the time of filing the tax return for the taxable year in which the number of full-time employees decreased: <Newly Inserted on Dec. 30, 2010; Feb. 2, 2012; Feb. 3, 2015>
  1. Where the number of full-time employees decreased only in one taxable year: The number of full-time employees decreased compared to that in the tax-credit granted taxable year under Article 26 (1) 2 or 144 (3) of the Act (referring to the second taxable year, if tax credits were granted for two consecutive taxable years) × Ten million won;
  2. Where the number of full-time employees decreased for two consecutive taxable years:
    (a) The first taxable year in which the number of full-time employees decreased: An amount calculated under subparagraph 1;
    (b) The second taxable year in which the number of full-time employees decreased: The decreased number of full-time employees between the immediately preceding taxable year and the relevant taxable year × Ten million won.
  (10) For the purposes of paragraphs (7) through (9), full-time employees shall be nationals who have concluded an employment contract under the Labor Standards Act: Provided, That the following persons shall be excluded herefrom: <Newly Inserted on Feb. 2, 2012; Aug. 31, 2012; Feb. 3, 2015; Feb. 13, 2018; Feb. 12, 2019; Feb. 11, 2020; Jun. 2, 2020>
  1. An employee whose employment contract term is less than one year (excluding employees whose total employment contract term is not less than one year due to consecutive renewal of an employment contract);
  2. A part-time employee defined in Article 2 (1) 9 of the Labor Standards Act: Provided, That an employee whose contractual work hours per month are at least 60 hours shall be deemed a full-time employee;
  3. An executive officer falling under any subparagraph of Article 40 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act;
  4. The largest stockholder or the largest investor of the relevant enterprise (referring to the representative, if the relevant enterprise is a sole proprietorship), and his/her spouse;
  5. A lineal ascendant or descendant (including the spouses) of a person referred to in subparagraph 4 or a relative as defined in Article 1-2 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on National Taxes, of such person;
  6. A person in whose case neither the fact that his/her earned income tax has been withheld nor the fact that any of the following amounts has been paid is verified by a book for tax withholding for earned income under Article 196 of the Enforcement Decree of Income Tax Act:
    (a) An employer contribution or employee endowment defined in Article 3 (1) 11 or 12 of the National Pension Act;
    (b) Premiums of employer-provided policy-holders under Article 69 of the National Health Insurance Act.
  (11) For the purposes of paragraphs (7) and (8), the number of full-time employees shall be calculated by the formula provided for in subparagraph 1. In such cases, one worker provided for in the proviso to paragraph (10) 2 shall be calculated as 0.5 person; where all of the requirements for support provided for in the items of subparagraph 2 are met, one worker shall be calculated as 0.75 person: <Amended on Feb. 21, 2014; Feb. 3, 2015; Feb. 5, 2016>
  1. Formula:
  Number of full-time employees = Sum of the number of full-time employees as at the end of each month in the relevant taxable year ÷ the number of months in the relevant taxable year
  2. Requirements for support:
    (a) The number of full-time employees in the relevant taxable year (excluding the workers provided for in the proviso to paragraph (10) 2) shall not be decreased as compared with that in the immediately preceding taxable year (excluding the workers provided for in the proviso to paragraph (10) 2);
    (b) They shall have entered into an indefinite employment contract;
    (c) They shall not be given discriminatory treatment defined in subparagraph 3 of Article 2 of the Act on the Protection of Fixed-Term and Part-Time Employees, in their hourly wages (including wages defined in Article 2 (1) 5 of the Labor Standards Act, regularly paid bonuses, such as regular bonuses and holiday bonuses, and performance-based incentives) and other matters concerning the terms and conditions of employment, welfare, etc.;
    (d) The hourly wage shall be at least 130/100 (or 120/100, in cases of a small or medium enterprise) of the minimum wage prescribed under Article 5 of the Minimum Wage Act.
  (12) The fraction of less than 1/100 of the number of full-time employees calculated under paragraph (11) shall be deemed nil. <Newly Inserted on Feb. 2, 2012; Feb. 3, 2015>
  (13) For the purposes of paragraphs (7) and (8), if a national starts a business, etc. in the relevant taxable year, the number according to the following classification shall be deemed the number of full-time employees in the immediately preceding taxable year or relevant taxable year: <Newly Inserted on Feb. 2, 2012; Feb. 15, 2013; Feb. 21, 2014; Feb. 3, 2015; Feb. 13, 2018; Feb. 12, 2019>
  1. Number of full-time employees in the immediately preceding taxable year where the national starts a business (excluding cases falling under Article 6 (10) 1 through 3 of the Act): 0;
  2. Number of full-time employees in the immediately preceding taxable year in any case falling under Article 6 (10) 1 (excluding succession to a previous business through a merger, split-off, investment in kind, or acquisition of business) through 3 of the Act: Number of full-time employees in the immediately preceding taxable year of the previous business, business before conversion into a corporation, or business before closure of business;
  3. Number of full-time employees in the immediately preceding taxable year or relevant taxable year in either of the following cases: In cases of an enterprise that has transferred the business, the number calculated by subtracting the number of full-time employees transferred by succession from the number of full-time employees in the immediately preceding taxable year; and in cases of an enterprise that has acquired the business, the number calculated by adding the number of full-time employees acquired by succession to the number of full-time employees of an enterprise succeeded in the immediately preceding taxable year; and the number of full-time employees in the relevant taxable year shall be the number of full-time employees calculated by deeming the full-time employees have been transferred or acquired by succession on the date the relevant taxable year commences:
    (a) Where such enterprise takes over full-time employees engaging in the previous business division by a merger, a split-off, an investment in kind, a transfer of business, or any similar event in the relevant taxable year;
    (b) Where such enterprise takes over full-time employees from a related party referred to in Article 11 (1).
  (14) For the purposes of paragraphs (1) and (2), the commencement date of an investment shall be any of the following applicable dates: <Amended on Oct. 7, 2008; Jun. 19, 2009; Feb. 18, 2010; Dec. 30, 2010; Feb. 2, 2012; Feb. 3, 2015>
  1. The date a person placing an order sends the first order sheet when placing orders under a domestic or international manufacturing contract;
  2. The date a down payment or a part of prices is paid (the date facilities are actually taken over, if such facilities have been taken over before a down payment or a part of prices is paid) where the purchases are made not by the order referred to in subparagraph 1, but under a sales and purchase agreement;
  3. The date an import license is obtained where a license is to be obtained to import the relevant facilities, notwithstanding subparagraphs 1 and 2;
  4. The date construction or manufacturing is actually started where he/she directly engages in construction or manufacturing. In such cases, those for the validity of business and preliminary preparation thereof shall not imply the time such construction or manufacturing is started;
  5. The date construction is actually started where construction is entrusted to any third person. In such cases, those for the validity of business and preliminary preparation thereof shall not imply the time when such construction is started.
  (15) Any person who wishes to be granted a tax credit under Article 26 (1) of the Act shall file an application for tax credits and a statement of calculation of tax credits in the forms prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, with the head of a tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment, along with his/her tax return. <Amended on Feb. 29, 2008; Jun. 19, 2009; Feb. 2, 2012; Feb. 3, 2015>
  (16) Any person who wishes to be granted a tax credit under Article 26 (2) of the Act shall submit an application for tax credits in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, to the head of a tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment, when making an interim tax prepayment. <Newly Inserted on Aug. 14, 2001; Feb. 29, 2008; Jun. 19, 2009; Dec. 30, 2010; Feb. 2, 2012; Feb. 3, 2015; Feb. 7, 2017>
  (17) Any person who wishes to file a return of an interim tax prepayment under Article 26 (3) of the Act shall submit an application for tax credits and a return of the interim tax pre-payment in the forms prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, to the head of a tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment. <Newly Inserted on Aug. 14, 2001; Feb. 29, 2008; Jun. 19, 2009; Dec. 30, 2010; Feb. 2, 2012; Feb. 3, 2015; Feb. 7, 2017>
  (18) Paragraphs (2) and (4) through (17) shall also apply mutatis mutandis to investments made under paragraph (1) in the Gaeseong Industrial District defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Act on the Support of Gaeseong Industrial District. <Newly Inserted on Mar. 10, 2008; Jan. 19, 2009; Feb. 3, 2015; Feb. 17, 2021>
  [Title Amended on Dec. 30, 2010]

Article 24 (Special Taxation on Investors in Specific Infrastructure Funds) (1)"Kind of the collective investment scheme prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 26-2 (1) 1 of the Act means a collective investment scheme referred to any of the following subparagraphs:
  1. A real estate investment company defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Real Estate Investment Company Act;
  3. Collective investment schemes under Article 9 (18) of the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act.
  3. A real estate fund under subparagraph 2 of Article 229 of the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act;
  4. A special asset fund under subparagraph 3 of Article 229 of the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act.
  (2)"objects of investment prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 26-2 (1) 2 of the Act means assets related to infrastructure and real estate (hereafter in this Article referred to as"specific infrastructure") that are recognized as related to the industry of item 2, among the assets of item 1, as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance (hereafter in this Article referred to as"investment objects"). <Amended on Mar. 25, 2022>
  1. Specific infrastructure-related assets
    (a) Stocks, equity shares, and bonds (including loan claims) under Article 43 (1) (1) and (2) of the Act on Public-Private Partnerships in Infrastructure;
    (b) Assets acquired pursuant to Article 43 (1) 3 or 4 of the Act on Public-Private Partnerships in Infrastructure;
    (c) Assets prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, such as real estate can be defined as infrastructure under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Act on Public-Private Partnerships in Infrastructure;
  2. Specific infrastructure-related industries:
    (a) Information and communications industry defined in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Information and Communications Technology Industry Promotion Act;
    (b) (b) Green industry under the Framework Act on Carbon Neutrality and Green Growth for Coping with Climate Crisis;
    (c) Other industries prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
  (3)"Proportion prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 26-2 (1) 2 of the Act means 50/100.
  (4) The proportion prescribed in paragraph (3) shall be the annual average ratio of the value of the investment objects in total assets of a specific infrastructure fund under Article 26-2 (1) of the Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as"specific infrastructure fund"), which shall be calculated according to the following formula. In such cases, the period for determining the annual average ratio shall be the period of one year from the date of creation, establishment date, or business license (hereafter this paragraph referred to as"establishment date or the like").
  Annual average ratio + A + B
  A: Aggregate of daily investment ratio Investment ratio = Value of investment objects in assets/Total assets of the specific infrastructure fund
  B: Total number of days during each year from the establishment date or the like
  (5) Where a specific infrastructure fund invests an investment object in assets through another collective investment scheme, the value of the investment object in the formula of paragraph (4) shall be calculated according to following formula:
  Value of investment assets in assets = A + B
  A: Value of target investment assets held by the specific infrastructure fund
  B: Value of equity securities or beneficiary certificates of another collective investment scheme held by the specific infrastructure fund × (Value of investment objects held by the other specific infrastructure fund/Total assets of the other collective investment scheme)
  (6) When calculating the daily investment ratio under paragraphs (4) and (5), if the value of target investment assets is less than the principal of investment amounts, the value of the target investment assets shall be calculated according to the following formula:
  Value of investment assets in assets = A + B
  A: Principal of investment amounts in target investment assets held by the specific infrastructure fund
  B: Principal of investment amounts in equity securities or beneficiary certificates of another collective investment scheme held by the specific infrastructure fund × (Value of investment objects held by the other specific infrastructure fund/Total assets of the other collective investment scheme)
  (7) Notwithstanding the formula of paragraph (4), the period falling under any of the following subparagraphs and the daily investment rate for that period shall be excluded from the calculation of the annual average rate:
  1. Three months from the establishment date of the specific infrastructure fund;
  2. Three months prior to the termination or dissolution of the specific infrastructure fund;
  3. Other period prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, such as a period of delay in an infrastructure project.
  (8) The specific requirements for a dedicated account under Article 26-2 (2) of the Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as"dedicated account") are as follows:
  1. The name of the account shall be a dedicated account of a specific infrastructure fund;
  2. The contract period shall be at least one year.
  (9) Dividend income and financial investment income paid to a dedicated account and reinvested amounts shall not be included in the maximum limit for deposit under Article 26-2 (2) 2 of the Act. <Amended on Feb. 15, 2022>
  (10) If a resident holding a dedicated account (hereinafter referred to as"account holder") withdraws any amount from the dedicated account, it shall be treated as a withdrawal from the principal of investment amounts.
  (11) Even where a contract is terminated during the contract period for any of the following reasons, the special taxation under Article 26-2 (1) of the Act shall apply to dividend income and financial investment income received at the time of termination. <Amended on Feb. 15, 2022>
  1. Where the account holder is dead or emigrates to a foreign country;
  2. Where the event referred to in any subparagraph of Article 81 (6) occurs to the account holder within six months prior to the date of termination;
  (12) A resident who intends to terminate the contract for any of the reasons listed in paragraph (11) shall submit a report on the special reason for termination, in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, to the financial company that manages the dedicated account (hereafter in this Article referred to as"financial company or the like").
  (13) The Ministry of Economy and Finance may establish a committee to review specific infrastructure projects.
  (14) A person who wishes to be granted special treatment under Article 26-2 (3) of the Act shall submit an application for aggregate taxation of financial investment income, in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, to the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment by the deadline for filing the final tax return on the tax base of financial investment income pursuant to Article 87-23 of the Income Tax Act. <Newly Inserted on Feb. 15, 2022>
  (15) The management of dedicated accounts, the composition and operation of the committee under paragraph (13), and other necessary matters shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance. <Amended on Feb. 15, 2022>
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Feb. 17, 2021]

Article 24-2 (Special Taxation for Investors in Collective Investment Schemes Specializing in Investment and Financing) (1) The specific requirements for a dedicated account under Article 27 (1) 1 of the Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as"dedicated account") are as follows:
  1. The name of the dedicated account shall be a dedicate account for a collective investment scheme for investment and financing under the provisions, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 27 (1) of the Act;
  2. The contract period shall be at least one year.
  3. The account shall be only for investments in collective investment securities under Article 9 (21) of the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act (hereinafter referred to as"collective investment securities") of collective investment schemes for investment and financing;
  4. The account shall be subject to restriction on the transfer of collective investment securities of collective investment schemes held prior to opening the dedicated account.
  (2) Dividend income and financial investment income paid to a dedicated account and reinvested amounts shall not be included in the maximum limit on deposit under Article 27 (1) 3 of the Act. <Amended on Feb. 15, 2022>
  (3) If an account holder withdraws any amount from the dedicated account, it shall be treated as a withdrawal from the principal of investment amounts.
  (4) Article 24 (11) and (12) shall apply mutatis mutandis to termination of a dedicated account during the contract period.
  (5) Deleted. <Feb. 15, 2022>
  (6) A person who wishes to be granted special treatment under Article 27 (2) of the Act shall submit an application for aggregate taxation of financial investment income, in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, to the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment by the deadline for filing the final tax return on the tax base of financial investment income pursuant to Article 87-23 of the Income Tax Act. <Newly Inserted on Feb. 15, 2022>
  (7) Except as otherwise expressly prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (6), matters necessary for the management of dedicated accounts, etc. shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance. <Amended on Feb. 15, 2022>
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Feb. 17, 2021]

Article 24-3 Deleted. <Dec. 29, 2000>

Article 25 (Special Taxation for Including Depreciation Cost of Service Businesses in Deductible Expenses) (1)"Service businesses prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 28 (1) of the Act, with the exception of its subparagraphs, means the service businesses specified in Article 23 (4).
  (2)"Fixed assets prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 28 (1) of the Act, with the exception of its subparagraphs, means the assets specified in Article 28 (6) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and the subparagraphs of Article 63 (5) of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act (hereafter in this Article, referred to as"assets invested in plant and equipment").
  (3)"Amount calculated by the formula prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 28 (1) of the Act, with the exception of its subparagraphs, means an amount (hereafter in this Article referred to as"allowable limit for depreciation") calculated by the method described in Article 26 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 64 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as"depreciation method") based on the reported service life under paragraph (4), notwithstanding the main clause of Article 28 (1) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and Article 63 (1) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act. In such cases, the depreciation method shall be based on the method reported by the relevant national in accordance with Article 26 (3) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 64 (2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act; and Article 26 (2) and (4) through (9) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and the latter part of Article 62 (1), Articles 64 (3) and (4), 66, and 71 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the methods for applying the deprecation methods and calculating a specific allowable limit for depreciation.
  (4) The service life applicable to calculating the allowable limit for depreciation shall be the number of useful years (hereafter in this Article, referred to as"reported service life") that the relevant national chooses within the range (a fraction of less than one year shall be deemed nil) determined by adding or subtracting 40/100 of the number of the standard service life specified in Article 26-3 (2) 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 63 (1) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act (hereafter in this Article, referred to as"standard service life") to or from the standard service life and reports to the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment. If the business year of a corporation is less than one year in such cases, Article 28 (2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the calculation of the service life.
  (5) A national shall continue to apply the reported service life which has been applied to each type of asset invested in plant and equipment and each type of business under paragraph (4), to subsequent taxable years.
  (6) Article 23 (2) of the Corporate Tax Act shall not apply to the assets invested in plant and equipment eligible under Article 28 (1) of the Act; and Article 29-2 (2) 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act shall apply only where the merging corporation, the corporation newly established as a consequence of a split-off, or the other corporation subject to a merger by division, engages in any of the businesses referred to in paragraph (1), and uses such assets for the relevant business, if the corporation acquires such assets through a qualified merger or a qualified split-off. <Amended on Feb. 13, 2018>
  (7) Where a national changes the depreciation method pursuant to Article 27 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 65 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act, the national shall calculate the allowable limit for depreciation for assets invested in plant and equipment by applying the changed depreciation method. In such cases, Article 27 (5) and (6) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and Articles 64 (5) and 65 (5) of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the method for calculating the allowable limit for depreciation.
  (8) Articles 30 through 32 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and Articles 62 (5) through (8), 67, 68, and 73 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis for the purposes of Article 28 (1) of the Act.
  (9) A person who intends to be accorded special taxation under Article 28 (1) of the Act shall separate assets invested in plant and equipment from other assets; prepare and keep statements of adjustment of depreciation cost in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance; submit a sum table of statements of adjustment of depreciation cost and statements of adjustment of depreciation cost in the forms prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, to the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment, along with a tax return; and file an application for special taxation for service life in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, with the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment, by the deadline for filing a tax return for the taxable year in which the person acquires such assets invested in plant and equipment (including filing through the national tax information communications network under subparagraph 19 of Article 2 of the Framework Act on National Taxes (hereinafter referred to as the "Home Tax Service Network")).
  (10) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (9), matters necessary for calculating the depreciation cost of assets invested in plant and equipment, shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Feb. 3, 2015]

Article 25-2 (Special Taxation for Including Depreciation Cost of Assets Invested in Plant and Equipment of Small or Medium and Middle-Standing Enterprises in Deductible Expenses) (1) Deleted. <Feb. 17, 2021>
  (2)"Fixed assets prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 28-2 (1) of the Act means the assets specified under Article 28 (6) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 63 (5) of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act (hereafter in this Article, referred to as"assets invested in plant and equipment").
  (3)"Amount calculated, as prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 28-2 (1) of the Act means an amount (hereafter in this Article referred to as"allowable limit for depreciation") calculated by the method prescribed in Article 26 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 64 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as"depreciation method") by applying the reported service life under paragraph (4), notwithstanding the main clause of Article 28 (1) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 63 (1) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act. In such cases, Article 26 (2) through (9) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and the latter part of Article 62 (1), Articles 64 (2) through (4), 66, and 71 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the methods for applying the depreciation method and calculating a specific allowable limit for depreciation.
  (4) The service life applicable to calculating the allowable limit for depreciation shall be the service life that a small or medium enterprise or a middle-standing enterprise chooses within a range (a period of less than one year shall be deemed nil) determined by adding or subtracting 50/100 of the standard service life to/from the standard service life under Article 26-3 (2) 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 63 (1) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act (hereafter in this Article, referred to as"standard service life") and reports to the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment. If the business year of a corporation is less than one year in such cases, the service life shall be calculated by applying mutatis mutandisArticle 28 (2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act.
  (5) The service life that a small or medium enterprise or a middle-standing enterprise applies to assets invested in plant and equipment for each asset and for each type of business in accordance with paragraph (4), shall be continuously applied to subsequent taxable years.
  (6) Article 23 (2) of the Corporate Tax Act shall not apply to assets invested in plant and equipment subject to Article 28-2 (1) of the Act; and Article 29-2 (2) 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act shall apply to such assets invested in plant and equipment, if they are acquired through a qualified merger or a qualified division. <Amended on Feb. 13, 2018>
  (7) Where a small or medium enterprise or a middle-standing enterprise changes the depreciation method in accordance with Article 27 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 65 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act, it shall calculate the allowable limit for depreciation for assets invested in plant and equipment by the changed depreciation method. In such cases, Article 27 (5) and (6) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and Articles 64 (5) and 65 (5) of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the methods for calculating the allowable limit for depreciation.
  (8) Articles 30 through 32 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and Articles 62 (5) through (8), 67, 68, and 73 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis for the purposes of Article 28-2 (1) of the Act.
  (9) A person who wishes to be eligible under Article 28-2 (1) of the Act shall prepare and keep a statement of adjustment of depreciation costs separately for assets invested in plant and equipment from other assets in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance; file (including filing through the Home Tax Service Network; hereafter in this paragraph, the same shall apply) an aggregate table of statements of adjustment of depreciation costs in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance and statements of adjustment of depreciation costs in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, with the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment, along with his/her tax return; and file an application for special taxation on the service life in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, with the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment, by the deadline for filing the tax return for the taxable year in which the person acquires the relevant assets invested in plant and equipment.
  (10) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (9), matters necessary for calculating the depreciation costs of assets invested in plant and equipment, shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance. <Amended on Feb. 17, 2021>
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Feb. 7, 2017]

Article 25-3 (Special Taxation for Including Depreciation Cost of Assets Invested in Plant and Equipment) (1) Deleted. <Feb. 17, 2021>
  (2)"Fixed business assets" in Article 28-3 (1) 1 of the Act means any of the following assets:
  1. Motor vehicles and transportation equipment: Provided, That they shall be limited to those used for transportation business or lease business for the purpose of lease;
  2. Ships and aircraft: Provided, That they shall be limited to those used for transport business or for lease business for the purpose of lease;
  3. Tools, instruments and furnishings;
  4. Machinery and equipment.
  (3)"Investment assets for innovative growth prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 28-3 (1) 2 of the Act means any of the following facilities: <Amended on Jul. 30, 2019; Feb. 11, 2020; Feb. 17, 2021>
  1. Facilities for commercialization of new growth engines;
  2. Facilities for research and testing and those for vocational training facilities, which fall under any of the following:
    (a) Facilities designated by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance as research and testing facilities for research and development;
    (b) Facilities designated by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance as facilities for vocational training for human resources development;
  3. An energy-saving facility that falls under any of the following:
    (a) Energy saving facilities specified by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, such as energy saving facilities, etc. under the Energy Use Rationalization Act (including those installed by companies specialized in energy saving under that Act under the condition of acquiring ownership after repaying the consideration in installments);
    (b) A gray water system defined in subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Act on Promotion and Support of Water Reuse, water-saving fixtures under subparagraph 30 of Article 3 of the Water Supply and Waterworks Installation Act, and water-saving devices under subparagraph 31 of that Article;
    (c) Facilities designated by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance as those for manufacturing parts, intermediate materials, or finished products for facilities that produce new energy defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Act on the Promotion of the Development, Use and Diffusion of New and Renewable Energy and renewable energy defined in subparagraph 2 of that Article;
  4. Productivity-enhancing facilities that fall under any of the following:
    (a) Facilities specified by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance as those in which investments are made to improve processes or automate and informatize facilities (including intelligent factory facilities for the management or improvement of production and manufacturing process of products based on data);
    (b) Facilities designated by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance as those established by using or applying advanced technology;
    (c) Computers and their peripherals, software, communication facilities, and other tangible and intangible facilities used to electronically manage the supply chain, including material procurement, production planning, and inventory management, with a depreciation period of two years or more.
  (4) Notwithstanding the main clause of Article 28 (1) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and Article 63 (1) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act, the amount calculated according to the method under Article 26 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and Article 64 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as the"depreciation method") shall be the amount (hereafter in this Article referred to as the"allowable limit for depreciation") calculated by applying the service life declared pursuant to paragraph (5). In such cases, Article 26 (2) through (9) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and the latter part of Article 62 (1), Articles 64 (2) through (4), 66, and 71 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the methods for applying the depreciation method and calculating a specific allowable limit for depreciation.
  (5) The service life applicable to calculating the allowable limit for depreciation shall be the number of useful years (hereafter in this Article, referred to as"reported service life") that the relevant national chooses within the range (a fraction of less than one year shall be deemed nil) determined by adding or subtracting 50/100 (or 75/100 in cases of assets under each subparagraph of paragraph (2), as acquired by a small or medium enterprise) of the number of the standard service life specified in Article 26-3 (2) 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 63 (1) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act (hereafter in this Article, referred to as"standard service life") to or from the standard service life and reports to the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment. If the period of business of a corporation is less than one year in such cases, the service life shall be calculated by applying mutatis mutandisArticle 28 (2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act. <Amended on Jul. 30, 2019; Feb. 11, 2020; Feb. 17, 2021>
  (6) A national shall continue to apply the reported service life which has been applied to each type of asset invested in plant and equipment and each type of business under paragraph (5), to subsequent taxable years.
  (7) Article 23 (2) of the Corporate Tax Act shall not apply to the assets invested in plant and equipment subject to Article 28-3 (1) of the Act; and Article 29-2 (2) 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act shall apply to such assets invested in plant and equipment, if they are acquired through a qualified merger or a qualified division.
  (8) Where a national changes the depreciation method pursuant to Article 27 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 65 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act, the national shall calculate the allowable limit for depreciation for assets invested in plant and equipment by applying the changed depreciation method. In such cases, Article 27 (5) and (6) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and Articles 64 (5) and 65 (5) of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the method for calculating the allowable limit for depreciation.
  (9) Articles 30 through 32 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and Articles 62 (5) through (8), 67, 68, and 73 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis for the purposes of Article 28-3 (1) of the Act.
  (10) A person who wishes to be eligible under Article 28-3 (1) of the Act shall separate assets invested in plant and equipment from other assets; prepare and keep depreciation adjustment statements in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance; submit a summary sheet of deprecation adjustment statements and deprecation adjustment statements in the forms prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance to the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment (including filing through the Home Tax Service Network; hereafter in this paragraph, the same shall apply), along with a tax return; and file an application for special taxation for service life in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance with the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment, by the deadline for filing a tax return for the taxable year in which the person acquires such assets invested in plant and equipment.
  (11) Except as provided in paragraphs (2) through (10), matters necessary for calculating the depreciation cost of assets invested in plant and equipment, shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance. <Amended on Feb. 17, 2021>
  [This Article Newly Inserted on Feb. 12, 2019]

Article 25-4 (Special Taxation for Including Depreciation Cost of Energy Saving Facilities in Deductible Expenses) (1)"Energy saving facility prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 28-4 (1) of the Act means a facility under any item of Article 25-3 (3) 3 (hereafter in this Article referred to as"energy saving facility").
  (2)"Amount calculated as prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 28-4 (1) of the Act means an amount (hereafter in this Article referred to as"allowable limit for depreciation") calculated by the method prescribed in Article 26 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 64 (1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as"depreciation method") by applying the reported service life under paragraph (3), notwithstanding the main clause of Article 28 (1) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 63 (1) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act. In such cases, Article 26 (2) through (9) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and the latter part of Article 62 (1), Articles 64 (2) through (4), 66, and 71 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the methods for applying the depreciation method and calculating a specific allowable limit for depreciation.
  (3) The service life applicable to calculating the allowable limit for depreciation shall be the number of useful years (hereafter in this Article, referred to as"reported service life") that the relevant national chooses within the range (a fraction of less than one year shall be deemed nil) determined by adding or subtracting 50/100 (or 75/100 in cases of energy saving facilities acquired by a small or medium enterprise) of the number of the standard service life specified in Article 26-3 (2) 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 63 (1) 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act (hereafter in this Article referred to as"standard service life") to or from the standard service life and reports to the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment. If the period of business of a corporation is less than one year in such cases, the service life shall be calculated by applying mutatis mutandisArticle 28 (2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act.
  (4) A person who intends to report the service life pursuant to paragraph (3) shall submit an application for the special application of the service life prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance to the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment by the deadline for filing the tax return for the taxable year in which the relevant energy-saving facility is acquired.
  (5) A national shall continue to apply the reported service life which has been applied to each type of asset and each type of business for energy saving facilities under paragraph (3) to subsequent taxable years.
  (6) Article 23 (2) of the Corporate Tax Act shall not apply to the energy saving facilities subject to Article 28-4 (1) of the Act; and Article 29-2 (2) 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act shall apply to such energy saving facilities, if they are acquired through a qualified merger or a qualified division.
  (7) Where a national changes the depreciation method pursuant to Article 27 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act or Article 65 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act, the national shall calculate the allowable limit for depreciation for energy saving facilities by applying the changed depreciation method. In such cases, Article 27 (5) and (6) of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and Articles 64 (5) and 65 (5) of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the method for calculating the allowable limit for depreciation.
  (8) Articles 30 through 32 of the Enforcement Decree of the Corporate Tax Act and Articles 62 (5) through (8), 67, 68, and 73 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis for the purposes of Article 28-4 (1) of the Act.
  (9) A person that wishes to be eligible under Article 28-4 (1) of the Act shall separate energy saving facilities from other assets and prepare and keep a depreciation adjustment statement in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
  (10) A person who wishes to be eligible under Article 28-4 (1) of the Act shall submit a depreciation adjustment statement under paragraph (9) and a summary sheet of the depreciation adjustment statement in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance to the head of the tax office having jurisdiction over the place of tax payment when filing a tax return.
  (11) Except as provided in paragraphs (2) through (10), matters necessary for calculating the depreciation cost of energy saving facilities shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
